      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Dietmar Rabich",
      "description": "View of the offshore wind farm, Borkum, Lower Saxony, GermanyThe beaches of Borkum are closely linked to the island’s development as a seaside resort and health spa. Originally, they were natural dune and sand landscapes that were constantly changing due to wind and tides.In the 19th century, the emergence of seaside resorts brought about a change. In 1834, Borkum was officially recognized as a North Sea resort. During this period, the first bathing facilities, beach chairs, and separate bathing areas for men and women were created. To protect the island and its beaches from storm surges, dunes were reinforced in the 19th century and later groynes and revetments were built. These coastal protection measures shape the appearance of the beaches today.In the 20th century, Borkum developed into a popular holiday destination. The north beach became the main bathing beach, while the extensive south beach is considered a nature and relaxation area.",
